window.READING_EMBEDDED_MD = {
  "5l-mvr": "# ⚡ Input Current Shaping of Five-Level Multiple-Pole VIENNA Rectifier Topologies with Reduced Component and Better Performance\n\n> **Authors**: Ooi H. P. Gabriel, Ali I. Maswood, Lim Ziyou, and M. Abhinava Chaitanya (*School of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Nanyang Technological University, Singapore*)\n> **Publication**: IEEE (978-1-4799-0224-8/13), pp. 900–905, 2013\n> **Source PDF**: [Input_current_shaping_of_five_level_Multiple_pole_VIENNA_rectifier.pdf](Reading/Input_current_shaping_of_five_level_Multiple_pole_VIENNA_rectifier.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Architectural Motivation\n\nMultilevel active front-end (AFE) rectifiers—such as the conventional three-level **VIENNA rectifier** and **Neutral-Point-Clamped (NPC) rectifier**—are widely deployed in industrial drives to regulate DC-link voltage while maintaining low input current Total Harmonic Distortion ($\\text{THD}_i$) and near-unity Power Factor ($\\text{PF}$). However, conventional three-level topologies face a fundamental trade-off:\n1. **High Switching Frequency Penalty**: Minimizing input inductor ($L_a, L_b, L_c$) volume and input current ripple requires very high PWM switching frequencies, which severely increases electromagnetic interference (EMI), $dv/dt$ stress, and semiconductor switching losses.\n2. **Limitations of Prior Low-Loss Schemes**:\n   - **(a) Low Conduction Period Control**: Reduces device switching stress but requires bulky input line reactors at narrow conduction angles.\n   - **(b) Resonant DC-Link Circuits** & **(c) Transformer-Assisted ZVS PWM**: Achieve Zero-Voltage Switching (ZVS) / Zero-Current Switching (ZCS), but add substantial control complexity, auxiliary components, and converter weight.\n\n### Key Innovation of the Proposed 5L-MVR Topology\nThis paper introduces a **Unidirectional Three-Phase Five-Level Multiple-Pole VIENNA Rectifier (5L-MVR)** requiring **only 6 active IGBT switches total** (two switches per phase: one in the **Inner Cell** $S_{x1}$ and one in the **Outer Cell** $S_{x2}$):\n- Synthesizes a **5-level stepped input voltage waveform** ($+V_{dc}/2, +V_{dc}/4, 0, -V_{dc}/4, -V_{dc}/2$), drastically reducing $dv/dt$ and input inductor size.\n- Employs a **Hybrid Modulation Strategy**:\n  - **Inner Cell Switches ($S_{a1}, S_{b1}, S_{c1}$)**: Operated at fundamental grid frequency using a **Fixed Switching Angle Firing Method ($\\alpha = 30^\\circ$)** triggered at grid voltage zero-crossings.\n  - **Outer Cell Switches ($S_{a2}, S_{b2}, S_{c2}$)**: Operated using **low-frequency (1 kHz) Carrier-Based PWM** (Level-Shifted PWM **LS-PWM** or Phase-Shifted PWM **PS-PWM**).\n- **Validated Performance**: Achieves **$\\text{PF} = 0.99$** and **$\\text{THD}_i = 3.43\\%$** at a low **1 kHz** switching frequency with a compact, low-cost power-balance control loop.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Grid[\"3-Phase AC Mains\"]\n        Vabc[\"Grid Voltages V_a, V_b, V_c\"] --> Labc[\"Small Input Inductors L_a, L_b, L_c\"]\n    end\n    subgraph MVR[\"5-Level Multiple-Pole VIENNA Rectifier (Per Phase x = a,b,c)\"]\n        Labc --> Outer[\"Outer Cell (Da7, Da8, Da9–Da12 + Switch Sx2)\\nDriven by 1 kHz LS-PWM / PS-PWM\\nMax Voltage Stress = V_dc / 2\"]\n        Outer --> Inner[\"Inner Cell (Da1–Da6 + Switch Sx1)\\nDriven by Angle Firing (α = 30°)\\nMax Voltage Stress = V_dc / 4\"]\n    end\n    subgraph DCLink[\"Split 4-Capacitor DC Bus & Balancing\"]\n        Inner --> Caps[\"DC-Link Capacitors C1, C2, C3, C4\"]\n        Caps --> Bal[\"DC/DC Voltage Balancing Circuit (Q1–Q4, L1, L2)\"]\n        Bal --> Load[\"DC Load (V_dc)\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![Fig. 1: Proposed unidirectional five-level multiple-pole VIENNA rectifier (5L-MVR) with the balancing circuit.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig1_topology.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Topology Hierarchy & Bidirectional Switch Configurations\n\nThe 5L-MVR is derived from a bidirectional multiple-pole multilevel diode-clamped hierarchy (Fig. 3), simplified for unidirectional power flow with two cascaded VIENNA cells per phase:\n- **Outer Cell**: Clamps the phase node to the outer DC rails ($+2E = +V_{dc}/2$ and $-2E = -V_{dc}/2$) via fast recovery diodes $D_{x7}, D_{x8}$ and bidirectional switch $S_{x2}$.\n- **Inner Cell**: Clamps the intermediate voltage levels ($+E = +V_{dc}/4$, $0$, and $-E = -V_{dc}/4$) via diodes $D_{x1}\\text{--}D_{x6}$ and bidirectional switch $S_{x1}$.\n\n![Fig. 2: Alternative configuration of proposed unidirectional 5L-MVR with various types of bidirectional AC switches.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig2_alt_switches.png)\n\n![Fig. 3: Circuit diagram of multiple-pole multilevel rectifier with the switching position.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig3_pole_diagram.png)\n\n| Cell Stage | Active Switch | Clamping Diodes | Modulation Scheme | Switching Frequency | Voltage Stress |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inner Cell** | $S_{a1}, S_{b1}, S_{c1}$ | $D_{x1}\\text{--}D_{x6}$ | Fixed Switching Angle Firing ($\\alpha = 30^\\circ$) | Grid Zero-Crossing (Fundamental) | **$V_{dc} / 4$** |\n| **Outer Cell** | $S_{a2}, S_{b2}, S_{c2}$ | $D_{x7}\\text{--}D_{x12}$ | Carrier-Based PWM (LS-PWM or PS-PWM) | **1 kHz** (Low Switching Loss) | **$V_{dc} / 2$** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hybrid Switching Schemes & Analytical Device Stress Equations\n\nAs illustrated in **Fig. 4**, the inner cell switches ($S_{a1}, S_{b1}, S_{c1}$) begin conducting at each zero-crossing point of the grid phase voltage with a fixed conduction angle $\\alpha = 30^\\circ$, while the outer cell switches ($S_{a2}, S_{b2}, S_{c2}$) are modulated by 1 kHz PWM throughout the $30^\\circ\\text{--}150^\\circ$ window.\n\n![Fig. 4: Two proposed switching schemes: switching angle firing method for Sx1 and PWM techniques for Sx2.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig4_switching_schemes.png)\n\n### Complete Analytical Equations (Eq. 1–5)\n\n1. **Maximum Current Through Inner Cell Switch ($S_{x1}$)** during the first conduction period ($\\omega t = 0^\\circ\\text{ to }30^\\circ$):\n$$i_{(sw1)\\max} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}V_i}{\\omega L \\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{M\\pi}{24\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(1)}$$\n\n2. **Voltage Transfer Ratio ($M$)** regulating the DC-link voltage $V_{dc}$ relative to RMS line-to-line grid voltage $V_i$:\n$$M = \\frac{V_{dc}}{\\sqrt{3}V_i} \\qquad \\text{(2)}$$\n\n3. **Maximum Current Through Outer Cell Switch ($S_{x2}$)** under unity power factor during $\\omega t = 60^\\circ\\text{ to }90^\\circ$:\n$$i_{(sw2)\\max} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}V_i}{\\omega L \\sqrt{3}}\\left(1 - \\frac{M\\pi}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(3)}$$\n\n4. **Maximum Current Through Inner Cell Diode ($D_{x1}$)** at $\\omega t = 90^\\circ$ (when $S_{a1}$ is off, $i_{(d1)\\max}$ equals $i_{(sw2)\\max}$):\n$$i_{(d1)\\max} = i_{(sw2)\\max} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}V_i}{\\omega L \\sqrt{3}}\\left(1 - \\frac{M\\pi}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(4)}$$\n\n5. **Maximum Current Through Outer Cell Diode ($D_{x7}$)** when both switches ($S_{x1}$ and $S_{x2}$) are turned off:\n$$i_{(d7)\\max} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}V_i}{\\omega L \\sqrt{3}}\\left(1 - \\frac{5M\\pi}{24\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(5)}$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Power Balance Controller & DC-Link Regulation (Eq. 6–9)\n\nThe closed-loop controller (**Fig. 5**) combines an outer **DC-Link Voltage Control Loop** (PI compensator) with an inner **Power-Balance Dynamic Current Control Loop**.\n\n![Fig. 5: Proposed power balance control technique for 5L-MVR.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig5_power_balance_control.png)\n\n### Power-Balance Reference Current Formulation\n\n6. **Instantaneous Phase Reference Current $i_a^*(t)$** (sum of Load Current Estimator component + Equivalent Capacitor Current component):\n$$i_a^*(t) = \\left[i_{\\text{load}}(t) + i_{C_{eq}}(t)\\right]\\sin(\\omega t) = \\left[\\frac{\\sum_{x \\in \\{a,b,c\\}} \\left[V_{xo}(t) - L_x\\frac{di_x(t)}{dt} - R_x i_x(t)\\right] i_x(t)}{V_{dc}(t)} + C_{eq}\\frac{dV_{dc}(t)}{dt}\\right]\\sin(\\omega t) \\qquad \\text{(6)}$$\n\n7. **Grid Voltage Peak Amplitude Detector ($V_{\\text{peak}}$)** (valid under both balanced and unbalanced grid conditions):\n$$V_{\\text{peak}} = \\left[\\left(\\frac{2}{3}V_{ao}(t) - \\frac{1}{3}V_{bo}(t) - \\frac{1}{3}V_{co}(t)\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}V_{bo}(t) - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}V_{co}(t)\\right)^2\\right]^{1/2} \\qquad \\text{(7)}$$\n\n8. **Supply Voltage Unit Sinusoidal Template**:\n$$\\sin(\\omega t) = \\frac{V_a(t)}{V_{\\text{peak}}} \\qquad \\text{(8)}$$\n\n9. **Open-Loop Transfer Function of Energy Storage DC-Link Model**:\n$$L(s) = K_p\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{T_i s}\\right) \\times \\frac{3V_m}{V_{dc}C_{eq}} \\times \\frac{1}{s + 6V_m / (V_{dc}C_{eq}R)} \\qquad \\text{(9)}$$\nwhere $K_p$ is selected via pole cancellation for stable DC bus regulation. Two 1 kHz triangular carrier waves are compared against the current error and combined through a logic **NAND gate** to drive $S_{x2}$.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Simulation Results: LS-PWM vs. PS-PWM, Waveforms & THD Analysis\n\n### Switching Angle Optimization ($\\alpha = 0^\\circ\\text{ to }50^\\circ$, Fig. 6 & Fig. 7)\n- **Power Factor (Fig. 6)**: Exceeds **$0.99$** for all inner-cell switching angles $\\alpha > 10^\\circ$.\n- **Input Current THD (Fig. 7)**: Reaches its minimum band between $\\alpha = 20^\\circ\\text{ and }30^\\circ$.\n  - **Optimal LS-PWM Point**: $\\alpha = 30^\\circ \\implies \\text{THD}_i = \\mathbf{3.43\\%}$, $\\text{PF} = \\mathbf{0.99}$.\n  - **Optimal PS-PWM Point**: $\\alpha = 25^\\circ \\implies \\text{THD}_i = \\mathbf{3.60\\%}$, $\\text{PF} = \\mathbf{0.99}$.\n\n![Fig. 6: Input power factor performance based on 1 kHz PWM techniques with the respective switching angle range between 0° to 50°.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig6_pf_vs_angle.png)\n\n![Fig. 7: THD of the input current based on 1 kHz PWM techniques with the respective switching angle range between 0° to 50°.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig7_thd_vs_angle.png)\n\n### Ripple Reduction & Harmonic Performance (Eq. 10–11, Fig. 8–10)\n\n10. **Input Current Ripple Approximation** over switching interval $D T_s$:\n$$\\Delta i_a(t) = \\int_0^{DT_s} \\frac{V_{ao}(t) - V_{am}(t) - V_{mo}(t)}{L_a}\\,dt \\qquad \\text{(10)}$$\n\n11. **Power Factor & THD Relationship**:\n$$\\text{PF} = \\frac{\\cos\\varphi_1}{\\sqrt{1 + \\text{THD}_i^2}} \\quad \\text{(reported in paper Eq. 11 as } \\frac{\\cos\\varphi_1}{\\sqrt{1 - (\\text{THD}_i)^2}} \\text{)} \\qquad \\text{(11)}$$\n\n![Fig. 8: Input rectifier line voltage V_ab (referred from node 'a' to node 'b').](Reading/figures/mvr_fig8_line_voltage_vab.png)\n\n![Fig. 9: Main phase voltage V_ao and current waveform i_a with power factor of 0.99.](Reading/figures/mvr_fig9_phase_voltage_current.png)\n\n![Fig. 10: Input rectifier current FFT spectrum (Fundamental 50 Hz = 7.04, THD = 3.43%).](Reading/figures/mvr_fig10_fft_harmonic_thd.png)\n",
  "vr-dpmsm": "# ⚙️ Vienna Rectifier Front-End Dual Three-Phase PMSM Drive with Synergetic Control\n\n> **Authors**: Takanobu Ohno (*University of Innsbruck*), Michael Haider (*Bern, Switzerland*), and Spasoje Mirić (*University of Innsbruck, Austria*)\n> **Publication**: 2025 14th International Conference on Renewable Energy Research and Applications (ICRERA), Vienna, Austria, pp. 788–796 (DOI: `10.1109/ICRERA66237.2025.11283706`)\n> **Source PDF**: [Vienna_Rectifier_Front_End_Dual_Three_Phase_PMSM_Drive_with_Synergetic.pdf](Reading/Vienna_Rectifier_Front_End_Dual_Three_Phase_PMSM_Drive_with_Synergetic.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & System Architecture\n\nConventional $3\\Phi\\text{ }400\\text{ V}_{\\text{rms}}$ industrial motor drives rectify the grid to an $800\\text{ V}_{dc}$ bus, requiring **1.2 kV power semiconductors** in both the front-end rectifier and back-end inverters. For **dual three-phase permanent magnet synchronous machines (dPMSMs)**—which feature two electrically isolated three-phase stator winding sets shifted by $30^\\circ$—conventional solutions use either two parallel 2-level inverters on a single $800\\text{ V}$ bus or two separate rectifiers, both requiring 1.2 kV switches and bulky electrolytic DC-link capacitors.\n\n### Key Innovations of the Proposed VR-dPMSM Drive\n1. **Single Vienna Rectifier + Two Series-Stacked Two-Level Inverters (Fig. 1)**:\n   - The Vienna Rectifier (VR) naturally partitions the DC link into two symmetric rails ($u_{pm}$ and $u_{mn}$, each $\\le 400\\text{ V}$), directly feeding the upper inverter ($S_{uph}\\text{--}S_{wpl}$) and lower inverter ($S_{unh}\\text{--}S_{wnl}$).\n   - **Exclusive Use of 600 V / 650 V Wide-Bandgap Devices**: Enables full implementation with **650 V SiC MOSFETs (Fig. 1a)** or **650 V GaN-HEMTs + GaN Monolithic Bidirectional Switches (GaN-MBDS, Fig. 1b)**, cutting manufacturing $\\text{CO}_2$ footprint by up to $10\\times$ and end-application volume by $30\\%$.\n2. **Film / Ceramic DC-Link Capacitors ($C_p = C_n = 10\\text{ }\\mu\\text{F}$)**: Partitioned DC bus and synergetic power flow minimize capacitance requirements.\n3. **Synergetic Control & 1/3-PWM Mode**:\n   - Coordinates the VR and stacked inverters across **1/3-PWM** (low speed), **2/3-PWM** (transition), and **3/3-PWM** (high speed) modes.\n   - In **1/3-PWM mode**, the VR switches only during **one-third of the fundamental period** (the middle phase crossing zero), **reducing VR switching losses by 2/3 (66.7%)**, while the stacked inverters shape the DC-link voltage to follow the grid line-to-line envelope using the mechanical inertia of the dPMSM!\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Grid[\"3Φ 400Vrms Grid\\n(f_g = 50 Hz, L = 100 μH)\"] --> VR[\"Vienna Rectifier Front-End\\n650V SiC-MOSFET or GaN-MBDS\\n1/3-PWM, 2/3-PWM, 3/3-PWM\"]\n    VR -->|Upper Rail u_pm (280–400V)\\nC_p = 10 μF| InvP[\"Upper 3Φ Inverter (650V SiC/GaN)\\nStator Winding Set p (i_uvw,p)\"]\n    VR -->|Midpoint m (i_m = 0)| Mid[\"Shared Neutral Point m\"]\n    VR -->|Lower Rail u_mn (280–400V)\\nC_n = 10 μF| InvN[\"Lower 3Φ Inverter (650V SiC/GaN)\\nStator Winding Set n (i_uvw,n, 30° shift)\"]\n    InvP --> Motor[\"Dual Three-Phase PMSM\\n25 kW, 3000 rpm, 79.6 Nm\"]\n    InvN --> Motor\n```\n\n![Fig. 1: Circuit diagram of the proposed Vienna rectifier-based dual three-phase PMSM (VR-dPMSM) drive system: (a) 600 V SiC-MOSFET implementation, (b) 600 V GaN-HEMT and GaN-MBDS implementation.](Reading/figures/vrdpmsm_fig1_circuit_sic_gan.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Operating Modes: 3/3-PWM (High Speed) vs. 1/3-PWM Synergetic (Low Speed)\n\n### 2.1 High-Speed Non-Synergistic Operation: 3/3-PWM Mode (Fig. 2a)\nAt nominal speed ($3000\\text{ rpm}$, $25\\text{ kW}$), elevated dPMSM back-EMF requires a boosted constant total DC-link voltage $u_{pn} = u_{pm} + u_{mn} = 760\\text{ V}$ ($380\\text{ V}$ per rail).\n- All three VR bidirectional switches ($S_a, S_b, S_c$) are pulse-width modulated across the entire $360^\\circ$ mains period (**3/3-PWM**).\n- Zero average midpoint current ($\\bar{i}_m = 0$) is maintained by injecting the **3/3-PWM Common-Mode Voltage (Eq. 1)**:\n$$u_{cm,3/3} = u_{\\text{mid}}\\left(1 - \\frac{|u_{\\text{mid}}|}{\\max(|u_{\\min}|, |u_{\\max}|)}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(1)}$$\nwhere $u_{\\max} > u_{\\text{mid}} > u_{\\min}$ are the sorted local-average VR switching node voltages $\\{\\bar{u}_{\\bar{a}}, \\bar{u}_{\\bar{b}}, \\bar{u}_{\\bar{c}}\\}$.\n\n### 2.2 Low-Speed Synergetic Operation: 1/3-PWM Mode (Fig. 2b)\nAt lower speeds (e.g., $1500\\text{ rpm}$, $12.5\\text{ kW}$), back-EMF is lower, allowing $u_{pn}$ to be lowered to follow the **6-pulse line-to-line grid voltage envelope** ($\\max(|u_{ab}|, |u_{bc}|, |u_{ca}|)$).\n- Within each $60^\\circ$ sector (e.g., **Sector 1**, $\\omega t \\in [60^\\circ, 120^\\circ]$ where $u_a > 0 > u_c$ and $u_b$ is the middle phase):\n  - Phase $a$ diode clamps to positive rail $p$ ($d_a = +1$, no switching!).\n  - Phase $c$ diode clamps to negative rail $n$ ($d_c = -1$, no switching!).\n  - **Only the middle phase switch $S_b$ is modulated** to impress the quasi-triangular middle-phase current $i_b$, while the upper and lower stacked inverters alternate every $30^\\circ$ to regulate $i_a$ and $i_c$ (with the third current fixed by KCL: $i_a + i_b + i_c = 0$).\n- **1/3-PWM Common-Mode Voltage (Eq. 2–3)**:\n$$u_{cm,1/3} = u_{cm,1/3,\\max} = u_{cm,1/3,\\min} \\qquad \\text{(2)}$$\n$$u_{cm,1/3,\\max} = \\frac{u_{pn}}{2} - u_{\\max}, \\qquad u_{cm,1/3,\\min} = -\\frac{u_{pn}}{2} - u_{\\min} \\qquad \\text{(3)}$$\n- **Universal Common-Mode Voltage Expression Valid Across 1/3, 2/3, and 3/3 PWM Modes (Eq. 4)**:\n$$u_{cm} = \\max\\Big(u_{cm,1/3,\\min},\\; \\min\\big(u_{cm,1/3,\\max},\\; u_{cm,3/3}\\big)\\Big) \\qquad \\text{(4)}$$\n\n![Fig. 2: Characteristic waveforms for (a) high-speed operation with fully boosted DC-link voltage by VR of 3/3-PWM mode at 25 kW, and (b) low-speed operation with partially DC-link current controlled by stacked inverters (1/3-PWM mode) at 12.5 kW.](Reading/figures/vrdpmsm_fig2_waveforms_33_13_pwm.png)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cascaded Control Structure & Appendix Reference Equations (Eq. 5–12)\n\nThe cascaded control loop (**Fig. 3**) features bandwidth separation of $1 : 5 : 25$:\n1. **Outer Speed Loop ($f_{\\text{bw,speed}} = 400\\text{ Hz}$, Fig. 3b.i)**: PI controller computes torque reference $T^*$ from $\\Omega^* - \\Omega$, yielding VR power reference $P^*_{VR} = \\Omega^* T^*$.\n2. **Intermediate DC-Link Voltage Loop ($f_{\\text{bw,voltage}} = 2\\text{ kHz}$, Fig. 3b.iii)**: Regulates $u_{pm}$ and $u_{mn}$ via PI controllers generating capacitor power demands $p^*_{c,p}$ and $p^*_{c,n}$. Subtracting these from $P^*_{VR}/2$ yields stacked inverter power references:\n$$P^*_{si,p} = \\frac{P^*_{VR}}{2} - p^*_{c,p}, \\qquad P^*_{si,n} = \\frac{P^*_{VR}}{2} - p^*_{c,n}, \\qquad T^*_{p,n} = \\frac{P^*_{si,p,n}}{\\Omega}$$\n3. **Inner Grid Current & Motor FOC Loops ($f_{\\text{bw,vr}} = f_{\\text{bw,dq}} = 10\\text{ kHz}$, Fig. 3b.ii & 3b.iv)**:\n   - Grid conductance reference: $G^* = \\frac{P^*_{VR}}{\\frac{3}{2}\\hat{U}_{ac}^2}$, phase current references $i^*_{a,b,c} = G^* u_{a,b,c}$, and switching-node voltage references $u^*_{\\bar{x}} = u_x - u^*_{Lx}$.\n\n![Fig. 3: Control structure of the VR-dPMSM drive system: (a) schematic with measurement points; (b) cascaded control diagram comprising (b.i) speed control, (b.ii) grid-current control, (b.iii) DC-link capacitor voltage control, and (b.iv) field-oriented control (FOC).](Reading/figures/vrdpmsm_fig3_cascaded_control_foc.png)\n\n### Universal DC-Link Voltage Reference Formulation (Appendix Eq. 5–12)\n\n5. **Master DC-Link Voltage Reference Across All Modes**:\n$$u^*_{pn} = \\max\\left(u^*_{pn,1/3},\\; u^*_{pn,3/3},\\; u^*_{pn,2/3,\\max},\\; u^*_{pn,2/3,\\min}\\right) \\qquad \\text{(5)}$$\n\n6. **1/3-PWM Line-to-Line Envelope Reference**:\n$$u^*_{pn,1/3} = u^*_{\\max} - u^*_{\\min} \\qquad \\text{(6)}$$\nwhere $u^*_{\\max} = \\max(u^*_{\\bar{a}}, u^*_{\\bar{b}}, u^*_{\\bar{c}})$ and $u^*_{\\min} = \\min(u^*_{\\bar{a}}, u^*_{\\bar{b}}, u^*_{\\bar{c}})$ **(7)**.\n\n8. **3/3-PWM Speed-Adaptive DC-Link Voltage Reference** (with $\\gamma = 1.2$ for $20\\%$ inductor control margin):\n$$u^*_{pn,3/3} = 2\\hat{U}_{\\text{nom}}\\frac{\\Omega^*}{\\Omega_{\\text{nom}}}\\cdot 2\\cdot\\gamma \\qquad \\text{(8)}$$\n\n9–10. **2/3-PWM Smooth Transition References**:\n$$u^*_{pn,2/3,\\max} = \\frac{2}{1 + \\frac{3\\hat{U}_{ac}^2 / 2}{|u^*_{\\max}||u^*_{pn,3/3}|}}\\,u^*_{pn,1/3} \\quad\\text{(9)}, \\qquad u^*_{pn,2/3,\\min} = \\frac{2}{1 + \\frac{3\\hat{U}_{ac}^2 / 2}{|u^*_{\\min}||u^*_{pn,3/3}|}}\\,u^*_{pn,1/3} \\quad\\text{(10)}$$\n\n11–12. **Common-Mode Voltage Boundary Constraints**:\n$$-\\frac{u_{pn}}{2} \\le \\bar{u}_{\\{\\bar{a},\\bar{b},\\bar{c}\\}} + u_{cm} \\le \\frac{u_{pn}}{2} \\quad\\text{(11)} \\implies -\\frac{u_{pn}}{2} - u_{\\min} \\le u_{cm} \\le \\frac{u_{pn}}{2} - u_{\\max} \\quad\\text{(12)}$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Simulation Parameters (Table I) & Seamless Mode Transition Results (Fig. 4)\n\n![Table I: VR-dPMSM Simulation Parameters.](Reading/figures/vrdpmsm_table1_sim_params.png)\n\n| Subsystem | Parameter | Symbol | Value |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Vienna Rectifier** | Grid voltage (line-to-neutral peak) | $\\hat{U}_{ac}$ | $325\\text{ V}_{\\text{pk}}$ ($230\\text{ V}_{\\text{rms}}$) |\n| **Vienna Rectifier** | Grid frequency / Switching frequency | $f_g$ / $f_{sw,vr}$ | $50\\text{ Hz}$ / $100\\text{ kHz}$ |\n| **Vienna Rectifier** | Input inductance / DC-link capacitance | $L_{\\{a,b,c\\}}$ / $C_p, C_n$ | $100\\text{ }\\mu\\text{H}$ / $10\\text{ }\\mu\\text{F}$ |\n| **Vienna Rectifier** | Total DC-link voltage range | $u_{pn}$ | $560\\text{ V to }800\\text{ V}$ ($u_{pm}, u_{mn} \\in [280, 500]\\text{ V}$) |\n| **Dual 3Φ PMSM** | Flux linkage / Stator inductance / Pole pairs | $\\hat{\\Phi}$ / $L_s$ / $N_{pp}$ | $0.25\\text{ Wb}$ / $2\\text{ mH}$ / $2$ |\n| **Dual 3Φ PMSM** | Moment of inertia / Nominal phase voltage peak | $J$ / $\\hat{U}$ | $0.0446\\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$ / $260\\text{ V}$ |\n| **Dual 3Φ PMSM** | Nominal torque / power / speed | $T_{\\text{load,nom}}$ / $P_{\\text{nom}}$ / $n_{\\text{nom}}$ | $79.6\\text{ Nm}$ / $25\\text{ kW}$ / $3000\\text{ rpm}$ |\n| **Control Loops** | Bandwidths (Speed / Voltage / VR Current / $dq$ Current) | $f_{\\text{bw}}$ | $400\\text{ Hz}$ / $2\\text{ kHz}$ / $10\\text{ kHz}$ / $10\\text{ kHz}$ |\n\n![Fig. 4: Simulation waveforms of the VR-dPMSM drive system accelerating from 1500 rpm to 3000 rpm across (b) 1/3-PWM, (c) 2/3-PWM, and (d) 3/3-PWM modes.](Reading/figures/vrdpmsm_fig4_dynamic_sim_waveforms.png)\n",
  "wpcb-copper": "# ♻️ From E-Waste to Resource: Identifying the Optimal Pre-Treatment for Copper Recovery from Waste PCBs\n\n> **Authors**: Moisés Gómez (*Tsinghua University*), Daniel Peña-Torres (*Aalto University*), Melanie Colet-Lagrille (*Universidad de Chile*), Katherine Jaramillo (*CNP Chile*), I.M.S.K. Ilankoon (*Monash University Malaysia*), and Xianlai Zeng (*Tsinghua University*)\n> **Publication**: *Resources, Conservation & Recycling*, Vol. 228 (2026) 108821 (DOI: `10.1016/j.resconrec.2026.108821`)\n> **Source PDF**: [optimal pre-treatment for copper recovery from waste PCBs.pdf](Reading/optimal%20pre-treatment%20for%20copper%20recovery%20from%20waste%20PCBs.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Urban Mining Context\n\n- **Global E-Waste Surge**: Reached **62 Mt in 2022** and is projected to hit **82 Mt by 2030** ($+2.5\\text{ Mt/yr}$), yet only **22.3%** is formally collected and recycled.\n- **Waste Printed Circuit Boards (WPCBs)**: Account for **3–7% (average 5%)** of total e-waste ($1.5\\text{--}2\\text{ Mt/yr}$) and contain **20–35 wt.% Copper** ($\\approx 280\\text{ kg Cu per tonne of WPCBs}$)—**10 to 26 times richer** than natural copper ores!\n- **2050 Cumulative Outlook**: Using Gompertz S-curve modeling, cumulative WPCB generation reaches **184.5 Mt by 2050** ($6.9\\text{ Mt/yr}$ annual in 2050), containing **$50.7 \\pm 3.4\\text{ Mt}$ of recoverable Copper** (more than **twice** the entire 2024 global primary copper mine production of $23\\text{ Mt}$), valued at **USD 592.9 Billion** (at Dec 2025 LME prices).\n- **Resource Efficiency**: Recycling Cu from WPCBs saves **75–95% of energy** and **up to 65% of water**, avoiding $>120\\text{ tonnes}$ of mined ore waste per tonne of copper.\n\n![Fig. 1: Characterisation of PCBs: (a) Average material content in PCBs (wt.%), (b) average material distribution treemap, (c) multi-layered FR-4 PCB cross-section, and (d) e-waste separation & 5 primary EC connection types (SMT, THT, Screw joint, Rivet, Socket pedestal).](Reading/figures/cu_p5_img1_2100x2640.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Forecasting Models (Logistic & Gompertz) & TOPSIS Methodology (Eq. 1–7)\n\n### 2.1 S-Curve WPCB Forecasting Equations (Eq. 1–2)\n1. **Generalised Logistic Growth Model** ($R^2 = 0.9962$, predicts $109.7\\text{ Mt}$ total e-waste by 2050):\n$$f_{\\text{Logistic}}(x) = \\frac{A_1 - A_2}{1 + (x / x_0)^k} + A_2 \\qquad \\text{(1)}$$\n\n2. **Gompertz Growth Model** ($R^2 = 0.9950$, preferred model aligning with 2030 UN projections; predicts $137.4\\text{ Mt}$ e-waste / $6.9\\text{ Mt/yr}$ WPCBs by 2050):\n$$f_{\\text{Gompertz}}(x) = A\\,e^{-e^{-k(x - x_0)}} \\qquad \\text{(2)}$$\n\n![Fig. 2: Outlook of WPCBs (2000–2050): (a) Logistic and Gompertz projections, (b) annual and cumulative WPCB quantities, (c) 2024 global mined copper (23 Mt) vs. cumulative recoverable WPCB copper by 2050 (50.7 Mt), and (d) sensitivity to PCB share in e-waste (3% to 7%).](Reading/figures/cu_p6_img1_2100x1453.png)\n\n### 2.2 TOPSIS Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (Eq. 3–7)\nEvaluates $m = 13$ pre-treatment pathways across $n = 23$ indicators grouped into **Technical (11)**, **Environmental (7)**, and **Economic (5)** classes (scored 1 to 5):\n3. **Vector Normalisation**:\n$$r_{ij} = \\frac{x_{ij}}{\\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{m} x_{ij}^2}} \\qquad \\text{(3)}$$\n4. **Weighted Normalised Decision Matrix**:\n$$v_{ij} = \\omega_j \\times r_{ij} \\qquad \\text{(4)}$$\n5–6. **Euclidean Distances to Positive-Ideal ($A^{\\text{ideal}}$) and Negative-Ideal ($A^{\\text{worst}}$) Solutions**:\n$$S_i^{\\text{ideal}} = \\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\left(v_j^{\\text{ideal}} - v_{ij}\\right)^2} \\quad\\text{(5)}, \\qquad S_i^{\\text{worst}} = \\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\left(v_j^{\\text{worst}} - v_{ij}\\right)^2} \\quad\\text{(6)}$$\n7. **Relative Closeness Coefficient ($C_i^{\\text{ideal}}$)**:\n$$C_i^{\\text{ideal}} = \\frac{S_i^{\\text{worst}}}{S_i^{\\text{ideal}} + S_i^{\\text{worst}}} \\in [0, 1] \\qquad \\text{(7)}$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Comprehensive Comparison of 13 WPCB Pre-Treatment Technologies (Tables 1 & 2)\n\n| # | Category | Pre-Treatment Method | Key Advantages | Limitations & Challenges | Typical Process Parameters & Disassembly Efficiency |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | **Mechanical** | **Manual Dismantling** | Lowest CAPEX/OPEX; simple small-scale deployment; **#1 in Scenario 4 ($C_i=0.579$)** | Low throughput; worker hazard (toxic fumes, lead, sharp edges) | Soldering irons / heat guns / chisels |\n| **2** | **Mechanical** | **Grinding Dismantling** | High EC separation speed; automation-ready | Component damage; high tool wear; hazardous dust | 4 diamond grinders ($1.5\\text{ mm}$, $5500\\text{ rpm}$, $1\\text{--}5\\text{ cm/s}$, 3 cycles) $\\to \\mathbf{98\\%}$ (Lee et al., 2012) |\n| **3** | **Thermal** | **Hot Air Heating** | Uniform heating; scalable industrial batching | Component oxidation & thermal stress | Cylindrical air heater + vibration ($265^\\circ\\text{C}$, $10\\text{ rpm}$, $8\\text{ min}$) $\\to \\mathbf{\\sim 100\\%}$ (Wang et al., 2016) |\n| **4** | **Thermal** | **Infrared (IR) Heating** | Localized energy-efficient desoldering | Uneven heating on reflective surfaces; thermal shock | IR heaters + steel brushes ($0.33\\text{ cm/s}$, $250^\\circ\\text{C}$, $70\\text{ s/PCB}$) $\\to \\mathbf{\\sim 94\\%}$ (Park et al., 2015) |\n| **5** | **Thermal** | **Laser Heating** | Ultra-high precision; minimal collateral damage | High CAPEX; skilled calibration; limited throughput | Targeted laser solder reflow + ventilation |\n| **6** | **Thermal** | **Hot Fluid Heating** | Uniform heat transfer via oil/molten salt/ionic liquid | Fluid contamination & disposal cost | Water-soluble ionic liquid $[\\text{BMIm}]\\text{BF}_4$ ($250^\\circ\\text{C}$, $12\\text{ min}$, $45\\text{ rpm}$) $\\to \\mathbf{\\sim 90\\%}$ (Zeng et al., 2013) |\n| **7** | **Thermal** | **Molten Solder Bath** | Fast simultaneous batch desoldering | High thermal energy; solder bath contamination & PCB warping | Molten solder wave/bath ($>230^\\circ\\text{C}$) |\n| **8** | **Chemical** | **Solder Dissolution** | Selective chemical leaching of Sn/Pb solder | Corrosive acids/oxidants; hazardous effluent treatment | $3.5\\text{ M }\\text{CH}_4\\text{O}_3\\text{S} + 0.5\\text{ M }\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ ($20^\\circ\\text{C}$, $45\\text{ min}$) or $2.5\\text{ M }\\text{HBF}_4 + 0.4\\text{ M }\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ ($35\\text{ min}$) $\\to \\mathbf{\\sim 100\\%}$ (Zhang et al.) |\n| **9** | **Chemical** | **Supercritical Fluids** | Eco-friendly solvent reuse; degrades resin & solder | Very high pressure/energy CAPEX & OPEX | Supercritical water / $\\text{CO}_2$ autoclave |\n| **10** | **Chemical** | **Organic Solvent Treatment** | Delaminates multi-layer FR-4 brominated epoxy | Toxic organic solvent fumes & effluent recovery | DMAc / DMSO / $1\\text{ M }\\text{NaOH}$ ($280^\\circ\\text{C}$, $15\\text{ min}$) $\\to \\mathbf{\\sim 100\\%}$ (Pinho et al., 2018) |\n| **11** | **Innovative** | **Automated Disassembly (AI & Robotics)** | **#1 in Scenarios 1, 2, 3 ($C_i = 0.549, 0.661, 0.587$)**; non-destructive, safe, selective | High initial AI training & robotics CAPEX | YOLOv3 computer vision EC/IC classification ($52\\text{--}100\\%$) + robotic desoldering (Lu et al., 2022) |\n| **12** | **Innovative** | **High-Voltage Electric Pulse Crushing (HVEPC)** | Selective fragmentation along material boundaries; low dust | High electrical pulse energy & specialized HV equipment | High-voltage electrical discharge liberation (Duan et al., 2015) |\n| **13** | **Innovative** | **Heated Centrifugation** | Combines thermal solder melting + centrifugal detachment | High setup cost; risk of fragile component fracture | Heated drum + centrifugal rotor |\n\n---\n\n## 4. TOPSIS Multi-Scenario Rankings & 33-Scenario Sensitivity Analysis (Fig. 3 & Fig. 4)\n\n| Scenario | Weighting Scheme ($\\omega_{\\text{Tech}}, \\omega_{\\text{Env}}, \\omega_{\\text{Eco}}$) | Top-Ranked Method ($\\max C_i^{\\text{ideal}}$) | Lowest-Ranked Method ($\\min C_i^{\\text{ideal}}$) | Strategic Takeaway |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Scenario 1** | Equal Importance ($1/3, 1/3, 1/3$) | **Automated Disassembly ($0.549$)** | Supercritical Fluids ($0.355$) | Best overall balance across all 23 indicators |\n| **Scenario 2** | Technical Priority ($2/3, 1/6, 1/6$) | **Automated Disassembly ($0.661$)** | Heated Centrifugation ($0.342$) | AI & Robotics excels in selectivity, speed & component integrity |\n| **Scenario 3** | Environmental Priority ($1/6, 2/3, 1/6$) | **Automated Disassembly ($0.587$)** | Solder Dissolution ($0.337$) | Avoids toxic acid effluents and high thermal emissions |\n| **Scenario 4** | Economic Priority ($1/6, 1/6, 2/3$) | **Manual Dismantling ($0.579$)** | Supercritical Fluids ($0.222$) | Low CAPEX/OPEX favors manual dismantling under tight budgets |\n\n![Fig. 3: Radar charts illustrating TOPSIS closeness coefficients across Scenarios 1–4.](Reading/figures/cu_p9_img1_2100x1634.png)\n\n![Fig. 4: One-At-a-Time (OAT) sensitivity analysis across 33 weighting scenarios (0% to 100% in 10% increments) showing rank crossovers between Automated Disassembly and Manual Dismantling.](Reading/figures/cu_p10_img1_2100x2032.png)\n",
  "power-supplies": "# 🔋 Switching Power Supplies A to Z (3rd Edition Add-On) — Resonant LLC/WPT, Dual Active Bridge, PWM Switch & Digital PID Control\n\n> **Authors**: Sanjaya Maniktala and Nicola Rosano (ISBN: `9798588905015`, 305 Pages, 5 Chapters)\n> **Source PDF**: [Power Supplies A to Z 3rd Edition Maniktala Rosano.pdf](Reading/Power%20Supplies%20A%20to%20Z%203rd%20Edition%20Maniktala%20Rosano.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Master Architecture & Chapter Overview\n\n| Chapter | Title | Core Engineering Focus & Analytical Highlights | Pages |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ch. 1** | **Resonant Power: LC to WPT & LLC** | Parallel/Series LC energy sloshing ($\\frac{1}{2}LI^2 \\iff \\frac{1}{2}CV^2$), First Harmonic Approximation (FHA), why WPT is a loosely-coupled LLC (and why secondary series $C_s$ \"double resonator\" fails!), resonant peak shift locus, 900W wide-input LLC design, Universal Scaling Curves ($m = L_2/L_1$), discrete & hybrid frequency/phase-shift modulators | pp. 8–77 |\n| **Ch. 2** | **The Dual Active Bridge (DAB)** | Isolated bidirectional DC-DC topology, transformer leakage inductance $L_{\\text{lkg}}$ energy transfer, 8-segment piecewise linear inductor current analysis (Case 1: $D_1+D_2 \\ge 1$ vs. Case 2: $D_1+D_2 < 1$), SIMPLIS & Mathcad validation, ZVS boundaries, 200 kW EV/grid DAB design | pp. 78–119 |\n| **Ch. 3** | **Small Signal Modeling (Guest Chapter)** | Vorpérian 3-terminal invariant PWM switch cell in Voltage Mode (VMC) & Peak Current Mode Control (PCMC), subharmonic instability & slope compensation ramp, audiosusceptibility $G_{vg}(s)$ & control-to-output $G_{vd}(s)$ in TINA/SPICE & Octave | pp. 120–185 |\n| **Ch. 4** | **Loop Compensation & Bench Bode Analysis** | Inductor re-initialization during load transients, voltage positioning, Type 1 / Type 2 / Type 3 analog compensators, $k$-factor vs. manual pole-zero placement, Buck/Boost/Buck-Boost VMC & CMC plant transfer functions, closed-loop bench Bode injection | pp. 186–238 |\n| **Ch. 5** | **PID and Digital Control Loops** | High-$Q$ LC post-filter ringing & conditional stability at light load, Laplace/Z-domain pole-zero splitting ($Q < 0.5$ vs. $Q > 0.5$), the **Grand Analogy** between capacitor impedance $Z_C(s) = R_{\\text{ESR}} + \\frac{1}{sC} + sL_{\\text{ESL}}$ and PID controller $G_{\\text{PID}}(s) = K_P + \\frac{K_I}{s} + sK_D$, **Q-Mismatch Resolution** | pp. 239–305 |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Chapter 1: Resonant Power — From LC Tank to LLC & Wireless Power Transfer (WPT)\n\n### 2.1 LC Tank Energy Exchange & First Harmonic Approximation (FHA)\nAt the natural resonant frequency $f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}$, inductive reactance $X_L = 2\\pi f_0 L$ matches capacitive reactance $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f_0 C}$, and stored energy sloshes continuously between $L$ and $C$:\n$$\\frac{1}{2}L I_{\\text{pk}}^2 \\Longleftrightarrow \\frac{1}{2}C V_{\\text{pk}}^2, \\qquad Z_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}}, \\qquad f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}$$\n\n![Figure 1.1: How inductors and capacitors store and return energy, and how they pair up.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_1_lc_energy.png)\n\n### 2.2 Why WPT Is an LLC — And the \"Double Resonator\" ($C_s$) Trap\nManiktala demonstrates that a Wireless Power Transfer (WPT) coupled-coil system is electrically identical to an **LLC resonant converter** where the magnetizing inductance is $L_m = k L_p$ and leakage inductance is $L_r = (1-k)L_p$:\n- Using the **First Harmonic Approximation (FHA)**, the AC equivalent load resistance reflected to the tank is:\n$$R_{ac} = \\frac{8}{\\pi^2} n^2 R_{\\text{load}} \\approx 0.8106\\,n^2 R_{\\text{load}}$$\n- **The WPT \"Double Resonator\" Mistake (Fig. 1.5 & Fig. 1.9)**: Adding a secondary-side resonant capacitor $C_s$ creates a 4-element double resonator with **two wildly shifting gain peaks** that cause frequency-modulated control loops to lose monotonic \"Direction of Correction\" (DoC) and lock up! Eliminating $C_s$ reduces WPT to a well-behaved 3-element LLC tank.\n\n![Figure 1.5: Equivalent diagram of LLC and (correct) WPT.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_5_llc_wpt_equiv.png)\n\n![Figure 1.7: Varying load in an LLC causes a resonant peak frequency shift depending on coupling.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_7_llc_freq_shift.png)\n\n![Figure 1.9: Mathcad plot of gain for an LLC without Cs (top: clean single peak) vs. with secondary-side capacitor Cs (bottom: chaotic double-resonator peak splitting).](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_9_llc_gain_plot.png)\n\n### 2.3 LLC Voltage Gain Equation & Resonant Peak Locus (900W Design Example)\nDefining normalized frequency $f_n = f_{sw} / f_r$, inductance ratio $m = L_p / L_r$, and quality factor $Q = \\frac{\\sqrt{L_r / C_r}}{R_{ac}}$, the LLC voltage gain magnitude is:\n$$M(f_n, Q, m) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{m} - \\frac{1}{m f_n^2}\\right)^2 + Q^2\\left(f_n - \\frac{1}{f_n}\\right)^2}}$$\nIn the **900W Wide-Input LLC Design Example** ($L_p = 57.2\\text{ }\\mu\\text{H}$, $C_p = 225.8\\text{ nF}$), operating slightly to the **right of the resonant peak** ($f_{sw} > f_{\\text{peak}}$) guarantees inductive tank current for **Zero-Voltage Switching (ZVS)** of the primary half-bridge MOSFETs.\n\n![Figure 1.11: The locus of the elusive resonant peak as load R varies.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_11_resonant_peak_locus.png)\n\n![Figure 1.12: 900W LLC Design Validation via Simulations.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_12_900w_llc_sim.png)\n\n![Figure 1.27: Universal LLC Design Curves (m = L2/L1) for target gain > 1.](Reading/figures/ps_fig1_27_universal_llc_curves.png)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Chapter 2: The Dual Active Bridge (DAB)\n\nThe **Dual Active Bridge (DAB)** places two active full bridges across a high-frequency isolation transformer with series leakage inductance $L_{\\text{lkg}}$. Power flow magnitude and direction are controlled by the primary duty ratio $D_1$ ($\\text{Angle 1}$), secondary duty ratio $D_2$ ($\\text{Angle 2}$), and inter-bridge phase shift $\\phi$:\n- **Single Phase-Shift (SPS) Power Transfer Equation** ($D_1 = D_2 = 1$, phase shift $\\phi \\in [-\\pi, \\pi]$):\n$$P_{\\text{DAB}} = \\frac{n V_{\\text{in}} V_{\\text{out}}\\,\\phi(\\pi - |\\phi|)}{2\\pi^2 f_{sw} L_{\\text{lkg}}}$$\n- **Piecewise 8-Segment Inductor Current Formulation**: In each switching period $T_{sw}$, the voltage across $L_{\\text{lkg}}$ steps through 8 distinct intervals ($V_{L,k} = V_{\\text{pri},k} - n V_{\\text{sec},k}$), producing 8 linear current segments $\\Delta I_k = \\frac{V_{L,k}}{L_{\\text{lkg}}}\\Delta t_k$ under steady-state volt-second balance ($\\sum_{k=1}^8 \\Delta I_k = 0$).\n\n![Figure 2.1 & 2.2: The Dual Active Bridge schematic and timing diagram.](Reading/figures/ps_fig2_1_dab_schematic_timing.png)\n\n![Figure 2.10: The eight linear current segments of Case 1 in the DAB.](Reading/figures/ps_fig2_10_dab_eight_segments.png)\n\n![Figure 2.24: SIMPLIS simulation of 200 kW DAB converter.](Reading/figures/ps_fig2_24_dab_200kw_sim.png)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Chapters 3–5: Small-Signal PWM Switch, Type 3 Compensation & PID Q-Matching\n\n### 4.1 Vorpérian 3-Terminal PWM Switch & Peak Current Mode Control (Ch. 3)\nReplacing the non-linear active/passive switch pair with the invariant 3-terminal **Active (A) – Passive (P) – Common (C)** PWM switch cell yields exact small-signal AC models for Buck, Boost, and Buck-Boost converters in both **Voltage Mode Control (VMC)** and **Peak Current Mode Control (PCMC)**.\n- In **PCMC**, subharmonic oscillation occurs for $D > 0.5$ unless stabilizing **artificial ramp slope compensation $m_a$** is added:\n$$m_a \\ge \\frac{1}{2} m_2 \\quad \\text{(for deadbeat response)}, \\qquad Q_p = \\frac{1}{\\pi\\left(m_c D' - 0.5\\right)}$$\n\n![Figure 3.6: AC three-terminal invariant PWM switch cell for Voltage Mode CCM.](Reading/figures/ps_fig3_6_pwm_switch_vm_ccm.png)\n\n![Figure 3.17: Universal Peak Current Mode Control (PCMC) small-signal model.](Reading/figures/ps_fig3_17_pcmc_small_signal.png)\n\n### 4.2 Plant Transfer Functions & Type 3 Analog Compensation (Ch. 4)\nFor a Voltage-Mode Buck converter, the LC output filter introduces a double pole at $f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}$ (with quality factor $Q_{\\text{plant}}$) and an ESR zero at $f_{\\text{esr}} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi R_{\\text{ESR}} C}$:\n$$G_{vd}(s) = \\frac{V_{\\text{in}}}{V_{\\text{ramp}}}\\cdot\\frac{1 + s R_{\\text{ESR}} C}{1 + s\\left(\\frac{L}{R_{\\text{load}}} + R_{\\text{ESR}}C + R_{\\text{DCR}}C\\right) + s^2 L C \\left(1 + \\frac{R_{\\text{ESR}}}{R_{\\text{load}}}\\right)}$$\n\n![Figure 4.23 & 4.24: Type 3 compensator equations, strategy, and Bode response.](Reading/figures/ps_fig4_23_type3_compensator.png)\n\n![Figure 4.31: Buck converter in Voltage Mode Control (VMC) plant transfer function summary.](Reading/figures/ps_fig4_31_buck_vmc_plant.png)\n\n### 4.3 Digital PID Control & Maniktala's \"Grand Analogy\" for Q-Mismatch Resolution (Ch. 5)\nTraditional Type 3 analog compensators place two real (or $Q=0.5$ coincident) zeros near $f_0$, whereas a lightly loaded LC post-filter exhibits a **high-$Q$ complex conjugate pole pair** ($Q_{\\text{plant}} \\gg 0.5$). This **Q-Mismatch** causes severe phase dip and **conditional stability / output ringing** at light loads (**Fig. 5.4 & 5.21**).\n- **The Grand Analogy (Fig. 5.31)**: Comparing the impedance of a real RLC capacitor branch with a PID controller transfer function:\n$$Z_{\\text{cap}}(s) = R_{\\text{ESR}} + \\frac{1}{sC} + s L_{\\text{ESL}} = \\frac{1 + s R_{\\text{ESR}}C + s^2 L_{\\text{ESL}}C}{sC} \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; G_{\\text{PID}}(s) = K_P + \\frac{K_I}{s} + s K_D = \\frac{K_I\\left(1 + s\\frac{K_P}{K_I} + s^2\\frac{K_D}{K_I}\\right)}{s}$$\n- **Exact Q-Matching PID Coefficients (Fig. 5.32)**: In a digital PID controller, $K_P, K_I, K_D$ are independent numbers and can synthesize **complex conjugate zeros with $Q_{\\text{PID}} = Q_{\\text{plant}} > 0.5$** that exactly cancel the resonant peaking of the LC filter:\n$$\\omega_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_I}{K_D}}, \\qquad Q_{\\text{PID}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{K_I K_D}}{K_P} \\implies K_P = \\frac{2\\pi f_0 K_{\\text{gain}}}{Q_{\\text{target}}}, \\quad K_I = (2\\pi f_0)^2 K_{\\text{gain}}, \\quad K_D = K_{\\text{gain}}$$\n\n![Figure 5.4: LC post-filter frequency response as load is varied (high Q peaking at light load).](Reading/figures/ps_fig5_4_lc_post_filter_q.png)\n\n![Figure 5.31: The full analogy between a capacitor impedance and a PID compensator for Q-matching.](Reading/figures/ps_fig5_31_pid_q_matching.png)\n",
  "orcad-pcb": "# 🖥️ Complete PCB Design Using OrCAD Capture and PCB Editor (2nd Edition, 2019)\n\n> **Authors**: Kraig Mitzner, Bob Doe, Alexander Akulin, Anton Suponin, and Dirk Müller (*Academic Press / Elsevier, 2019, 588 Pages*)\n> **Source Folder**: `Reading/Complete PCB Design Using OrCAD Capture and PCB Editor/` (All 12 Chapters + Appendices A–E)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Complete Book Structure & Direct Chapter PDF Links\n\n| Ch / App | Title | Core Engineering & CAD Topics | Direct PDF Link |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ch. 1** | **Introduction to Printed Circuit Board Design** | FR-4 cores & prepreg, copper foil weights ($0.5\\text{--}2\\text{ oz}$), 2/4/6-layer & HDI build-up stackups, blind/buried/through vias, surface finishes (HASL, ENIG, OSP, Immersion Ag/Sn) | [Chapter 1 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_1_Introduction_to_printed_circui_2019_Complete_PCB_Design.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 2** | **Introduction to the PCB Design Flow by Example** | End-to-end schematic-to-Gerber walkthrough: Capture project wizard, part placement, netlisting, board outline, DRC, routing, and artwork | [Chapter 2 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_2_Introduction_to_the_printed_c_2019_Complete_PCB_Design.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 3** | **Project Structures & the OrCAD Capture Environment** | Directory hierarchy, `.opj`/`.dsn`/`.olb`/`.brd` files, Capture vs. PSpice libraries, flat vs. hierarchical schematics, PCB Editor UI panes | [Chapter 3 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_3_Project_structures_and_t_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 4** | **Introduction to Industry Standards** | IPC Class 1/2/3, IPC-2221/7351/A-600/A-610, standard panel sizes (A1–D4), fabrication tolerances, annular ring breakout, trace width vs. current | [Chapter 4 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_4_Introduction_to_indu_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 5** | **Introduction to Design for Manufacturing, Assembly & Test** | Wave vs. reflow soldering, component orientation, tombstoning & thermal relief pads, DFM/DFA spacing tables (Tables 5.1–5.7), ICT bed-of-nails DFT | [Chapter 5 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_5_Introduction_to_design_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 6** | **PCB Design for Signal Integrity, Power Integrity & EMI** | Loop inductance, return-current image planes, split-plane gap penalties, crosstalk ($3W$ rule), microstrip & stripline $Z_0$ equations, reflections, decoupling capacitor ESL/SRF | [Chapter 6 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_6_PCB_design_for_sign_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 7** | **Making and Editing Capture Parts** | Homogeneous vs. heterogeneous multi-section parts, pin electrical types, spreadsheet part creation, IEEE/ANSI symbol graphics | [Chapter 7 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_7_Making_and_editing_C_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 8** | **Making and Editing PCB Footprints** | Padstack Editor (`.pad`), regular/thermal/anti-pad layers, `.dra`/`.psm`/`.bsm`/`.ssm`/`.fsm` symbols, IPC-7351 land patterns, Package Symbol Wizard | [Chapter 8 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_8_Making_and_editing_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 9** | **Printed Circuit Board Layout** | 4 Complete Design Examples: Analog op-amp, mixed-signal PIC/ADC with split/moated planes, multilayer high-speed digital DDR/PCI, differential pairs & length tuning | [Chapter 9 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_9_Printed_circuit_board_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 10** | **Artwork Development and Gerber Files** | RS-274X vs. Gerber X2 & ODB++, NC Drill files, aperture tables, silkscreen clipping, backdrilling, panelization, fab drawing & BOM | [Chapter 10 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_10_Artwork_development_an_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 11** | **Component Information System (CIS)** | Relational SQL/Access component database, ODBC setup, `.dbc` configuration, `allegro.cfg` property mapping, Part Manager variant BOMs | [Chapter 11 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_11_Component_informa_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) |\n| **Ch. 12** | **Signal Integrity Simulation** | Sigrity / Topology Explorer pre-layout & post-layout SI workflow, IBIS buffer models, eye diagrams, series/parallel termination optimization | [Chapter 12 PDF](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Chapter_12_Signal_integrity_simu_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using.pdf) |\n| **App. A–E** | **Reference Appendices** | [App. A: Standards](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Appendix_A_List_of_design_s_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) · [App. B: Packages](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Appendix_B_Partial_list_of_packages_and_footp_2019_Complete_PCB.pdf) · [App. C: Logic Rise/Fall Times](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Appendix_C_Rise_and_fall_times_for_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using.pdf) · [App. D: Drill & Screw Sizes](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Appendix_D_Drill_and_screw_d_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD.pdf) | [App. E: References](Reading/Complete%20PCB%20Design%20Using%20OrCAD%20Capture%20and%20PCB%20Editor/Appendix_E_References_by_2019_Complete_PCB_Design_Using_OrCAD_Capture.pdf) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. PCB Stackups, Manufacturing Tolerances & DFM/DFA (Chapters 1–5)\n\n### 2.1 Multilayer Stackup Construction (Ch. 1)\nA multilayer PCB alternates fully cured **Copper-Clad Laminate Cores** (C-stage FR-4) with uncured **Prepreg** sheets (B-stage fiberglass impregnated with epoxy resin) pressed under heat and vacuum.\n- **Standard Copper Weights**: $0.5\\text{ oz/ft}^2$ ($17.5\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ / $0.7\\text{ mil}$), $1.0\\text{ oz/ft}^2$ ($35\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ / $1.4\\text{ mil}$), $2.0\\text{ oz/ft}^2$ ($70\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ / $2.8\\text{ mil}$).\n\n![Figure 1.3 & 1.4: Cores, prepreg, and two stack-up methods for a 6-layer board (foil-lamination vs. cap-lamination).](Reading/figures/orcad_ch1_stackups_6layer.png)\n\n![Figure 2.1: Complete OrCAD Capture to PCB Editor end-to-end design flow.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch2_design_flow.png)\n\n![Figure 3.1: OrCAD Capture & PCB Editor project design flow and options.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch3_project_flow.png)\n\n### 2.2 Annular Ring Tolerance & Soldering DFM (Ch. 4 & Ch. 5)\nMinimum pad diameter $D_{\\text{pad}}$ must account for maximum finished hole diameter $D_{\\text{hole,max}}$, minimum required annular ring $AR_{\\min}$ (e.g., $2\\text{ mil}$ Class 2, $0\\text{ mil}$ no-breakout Class 1), and total fabrication registration allowance $T_{\\text{fab}}$ ($\\approx 8\\text{--}14\\text{ mil}$):\n$$D_{\\text{pad,min}} = D_{\\text{drill}} + 2\\cdot AR_{\\min} + T_{\\text{fab}}$$\n\n![Figure 4.1 & 4.2: Fabrication tolerance, hole location uncertainty, and PTH annular ring breakout.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch4_pth_annular_breakout.png)\n\n![Figure 5.1 & 5.2: Wave soldering side and rear views for DFM component orientation.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch5_wave_reflow_soldering.png)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Signal Integrity, Power Integrity & EMI Equations (Chapters 6 & 12)\n\n### 3.1 Loop Inductance, Return Paths & Crosstalk (Ch. 6)\n- **High-Frequency Return Current Path**: Above a few kHz ($Z = R + j\\omega L$), return current on a ground plane does not take the path of least resistance—it flows **directly underneath the signal trace** to minimize loop area $A$ and loop inductance $L_{\\text{loop}}$. Routing a high-speed trace across a split-plane slot forces the return current around a large perimeter loop, creating a dipole EMI antenna and severe inductive ground bounce $V_{\\text{noise}} = L\\frac{di}{dt}$.\n- **Inductive & Capacitive Crosstalk ($3W$ Rule)**: Maintaining center-to-center trace spacing $S \\ge 3W$ (edge-to-edge $\\ge 2W$) reduces near-end/far-end crosstalk by $>70\\%$:\n$$\\text{CT}_{\\text{dB}} \\approx 20\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + (D / H)^2}\\right)$$\n\n![Figure 6.5 & 6.6: Closed-loop circuit with low loop inductance and aiding vs. opposing magnetic fields.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch6_loop_inductance_fields.png)\n\n### 3.2 Controlled Impedance Transmission Lines (Ch. 6 & Ch. 12)\n1. **Propagation Delay & Critical Trace Length ($l_{\\text{crit}}$)**:\n$$t_{pd} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}}}}{c} \\approx 85\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}}}\\text{ ps/in} \\quad\\left(\\approx 3.34\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}}}\\text{ ps/mm}\\right), \\qquad l_{\\text{crit}} = \\frac{t_r}{2\\,t_{pd}}$$\nWhenever trace length $l \\ge l_{\\text{crit}}$ (where $t_r$ is the logic family rise time from **Appendix C**), transmission-line termination is mandatory to prevent ringing and reflections:\n$$\\Gamma_{\\text{load}} = \\frac{Z_L - Z_0}{Z_L + Z_0}, \\qquad \\Gamma_{\\text{source}} = \\frac{Z_S - Z_0}{Z_S + Z_0}$$\n\n2. **Surface Microstrip Characteristic Impedance ($Z_0$)**:\n$$Z_{0,\\text{microstrip}} = \\frac{87}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r + 1.41}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5.98\\,h}{0.8\\,w + t}\\right)\\;\\Omega, \\qquad \\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_r + 1}{2} + \\frac{\\varepsilon_r - 1}{2}\\left(1 + \\frac{12\\,h}{w}\\right)^{-1/2}$$\n\n3. **Symmetric Stripline Characteristic Impedance ($Z_0$)**:\n$$Z_{0,\\text{stripline}} = \\frac{60}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{1.9\\,(2h + t)}{0.8\\,w + t}\\right)\\;\\Omega = \\frac{60}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{4\\,b}{0.67\\,\\pi(0.8\\,w + t)}\\right)\\;\\Omega$$\n\n4. **Decoupling Capacitor Self-Resonant Frequency (SRF)**:\n$$Z_{\\text{cap}}(f) = \\sqrt{R_{\\text{ESR}}^2 + \\left(2\\pi f L_{\\text{ESL}} - \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}\\right)^2}, \\qquad f_{\\text{SRF}} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L_{\\text{ESL}} C}}$$\nAbove $f_{\\text{SRF}}$, a decoupling capacitor behaves as an inductor ($+20\\text{ dB/dec}$) dominated by package + via loop inductance $L_{\\text{ESL}}$.\n\n![Figure 6.13 & 6.14: Surface microstrip and symmetric stripline geometry & impedance equations.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch6_microstrip_stripline.png)\n\n![Figure 7.5: Homogeneous vs. heterogeneous multi-section schematic parts in OrCAD Capture.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch7_homogeneous_heterogeneous.png)\n\n![Figure 8.2 & 8.3: Anatomy of a multilayer padstack in PCB Editor.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch8_padstack_anatomy.png)\n\n![Figure 9.1: Complete analog & mixed-signal PCB design example in OrCAD.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch9_analog_design_example.png)\n\n![Figure 10.3: Routed PCB board prepared for Gerber artwork generation.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch10_routed_board_gerber.png)\n\n![Figure 11.1: Overview of Component Information System (CIS) database architecture.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch11_cis_architecture.png)\n\n![Figure 12.5 & 12.6: Pre-layout transmission line reflection waveforms at sender and receiver.](Reading/figures/orcad_ch12_si_transmission_waveforms.png)\n",
  "ipc-610e": "# 🔍 IPC-A-610E: Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies — Complete Class 1, 2 & 3 Visual Inspection & Criteria Reference\n\n> **Standard**: ANSI/IPC-A-610E (*Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies*, 417 Pages, Sections 1–12 + Appendix A)\n> **Source PDF**: [561401556-IPC-A-610E.pdf](Reading/561401556-IPC-A-610E.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. IPC Product Classes & Four-Tier Inspection Disposition Hierarchy (Section 1)\n\n### 1.1 Three Product Classifications (Section 1.3)\n| Class | Designation | Typical End-Use Products | Reliability & Inspection Philosophy |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Class 1** | **General Electronic Products** | Consumer electronics, toys, low-cost peripherals | Cosmetic imperfections acceptable; primary requirement is **electrical & mechanical function** of the completed assembly. |\n| **Class 2** | **Dedicated Service Electronic Products** | Industrial automation, telecom infrastructure, servers, test instruments | Extended life and uninterrupted service desired, though non-critical environment; some cosmetic/dimensional latitude allowed. |\n| **Class 3** | **High Performance / Harsh Environment Electronic Products** | Aerospace, avionics, military defense, implantable/life-support medical, automotive safety | **Continued performance or performance-on-demand is critical**; equipment downtime cannot be tolerated; stringent dimensional & wetting limits. |\n\n### 1.2 Four Levels of Acceptance Criteria (Section 1.4.1)\n1. **Target Condition**: Close-to-perfect/preferred condition; desirable goal but not mandatory for service acceptance.\n2. **Acceptable Condition**: Assembly meets reliability requirements for its designated Class (1, 2, or 3) and must be accepted.\n3. **Process Indicator Condition**: Material/workmanship anomaly that **does not affect form, fit, function, or reliability** (therefore *not* a defect and shall not be rejected), but indicates process variation that should be monitored/corrected in statistical process control (SPC).\n4. **Defect Condition**: Insufficient form, fit, function, or reliability for the designated Class; **must be reworked, repaired, scrapped, or dispositioned via MRB**.\n\n### 1.3 Inspection Magnification Rules (Section 1.9, Table 1-2)\n| Land Width / Feature Size | Inspection Magnification | Referee Magnification (Max) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $> 1.0\\text{ mm}$ ($0.0394\\text{ in}$) | $1.75\\times$ | $4\\times$ |\n| $0.5\\text{ to }1.0\\text{ mm}$ ($0.0197\\text{--}0.0394\\text{ in}$) | $4\\times$ | $10\\times$ |\n| $0.25\\text{ to }0.5\\text{ mm}$ ($0.00984\\text{--}0.0197\\text{ in}$) | $10\\times$ | $20\\times$ |\n| $< 0.25\\text{ mm}$ ($0.00984\\text{ in}$) | $20\\times$ | $40\\times$ |\n\n![Section 1.5: Primary Side vs. Secondary Side and Solder Source Side vs. Solder Destination Side definitions.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec1_board_orientation.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. EOS/ESD Protection, Hardware Installation & Soldering Anomalies (Sections 3–5)\n\n- **Section 3 (EOS/ESD Prevention)**: EPA workstation wrist-strap ($1\\text{ M}\\Omega$ current-limiting resistor), dissipative mat ($10^6\\text{--}10^9\\text{ }\\Omega/\\text{sq}$), relative humidity $>30\\%$, no static-generating plastics/styrofoam within $30\\text{ cm}$ ($12\\text{ in}$) of ESDS assemblies.\n- **Section 4 (Hardware Installation)**:\n  - **Threaded Fasteners**: Minimum **1.5 threads** protruding beyond locking nut (up to $3\\text{ mm}$ plus 1 thread max unless interfering). Split-ring lock washers must be compressed flat with no cracking.\n  - **Heatsinks**: Must be flush and uniformly coated with thermal interface compound without contaminating solderable pads.\n- **Section 5 (Soldering Acceptability & Anomalies)**:\n  - **Nonwetting / Dewetting (Sec 5.2.4 / 5.2.6)**: Defect across Classes 1, 2, 3 whenever required minimum solder joint wetting criteria are violated.\n  - **Solder Balls / Fines (Sec 5.2.7.1)**: Defect (Class 1, 2, 3) if **unattached/loose** OR if violating minimum electrical clearance ($<0.13\\text{ mm}$ / $5\\text{ mil}$ or $>5$ balls within $600\\text{ mm}^2$ in No-Clean processes).\n  - **Lead-Free (SAC305) Appearance (Sec 5.1)**: Dull, matte, grainy, and slightly higher contact angle than Sn63Pb37 are **normal acceptable characteristics** of Pb-free alloys; inspection criteria for wetting and dimensional fill are **identical** for SnPb and Pb-free!\n\n![Section 3: EOS/ESD Safe Workstation (EPA) grounding and handling.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec3_eos_esd_workstation.png)\n\n![Section 4: Hardware installation and heatsink thermal compound contact.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec4_hardware_heatsinks.png)\n\n![Section 5: Soldering acceptability — SnPb vs. Lead-Free (SAC) appearance and wetting angle.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec5_soldering_acceptability.png)\n\n![Section 5.2.6: Dewetting and Nonwetting defect conditions.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec5_dewetting_nonwetting.png)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Terminal Connections & Through-Hole Technology (Sections 6 & 7)\n\n### 3.1 Terminal Wire Wrap & Solder Criteria (Section 6)\n| Terminal Type | Class 1 Wrap | Class 2 Wrap | Class 3 Wrap | Wetting Requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Turret & Straight Pin (6.8)** | $\\ge 90^\\circ$ (or mechanical secure) | $\\ge 180^\\circ$ | $\\ge 180^\\circ$ ($\\le 360^\\circ$ no overlap) | Visible wetted fillet along wire-to-post contact ($\\ge 75\\%$ Class 2/3) |\n| **Bifurcated Terminal (6.9)** | $\\ge 90^\\circ$ (side/bottom route) | $\\ge 90^\\circ$ | $\\ge 90^\\circ$ past post edge | Wire sits flush against base or previous turn; $\\ge 75\\%$ fillet |\n| **Hook Terminal (6.12)** | $\\ge 90^\\circ$ | $\\ge 180^\\circ$ | $\\ge 180^\\circ$ to $270^\\circ$ | No wire extending past hook tip by $>1$ wire diameter |\n| **Pierced / Perforated (6.11)** | Pass through eye | $>90^\\circ$ contact | $\\ge 180^\\circ$ contact | Wire contacts almeno 2 surfaces of terminal |\n| **Solder Cup (6.13)** | Inserted to full depth | Full depth, $\\le 1\\text{ dia}$ gap | Full depth, flush to back wall | Solder fills cup ($\\ge 75\\%$ Class 2, $100\\%$ Class 3) without excess wick |\n\n![Section 6.8: Turret and Straight Pin terminal wire placement and soldering.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec6_turret_terminals.png)\n\n### 3.2 Supported Plated-Through Hole (PTH) Soldering Criteria (Section 7.3.5, Table 7-4)\n| PTH Parameter | Symbol | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A. Vertical Solder Fill** | $A$ | Not specified (wetting evident) | **$\\ge 75\\%$** (Up to $25\\%$ recession total between primary & secondary; $\\ge 50\\%$ for heavy thermal planes) | **$\\ge 75\\%$** (Up to $25\\%$ recession total) |\n| **B. Primary Side (Destination) Lead-to-Barrel Wetting** | $B$ | Wetting evident | **$\\ge 180^\\circ$** | **$\\ge 270^\\circ$** |\n| **C. Primary Side Land Area Coverage** | $C$ | $0\\%$ | $0\\%$ (wetted in barrel) | $0\\%$ |\n| **D. Secondary Side (Source) Lead-to-Barrel Wetting** | $D$ | **$\\ge 270^\\circ$** | **$\\ge 270^\\circ$** | **$\\ge 330^\\circ$** |\n| **E. Secondary Side Land Area Coverage** | $E$ | **$\\ge 75\\%$** | **$\\ge 75\\%$** | **$\\ge 75\\%$** |\n| **Lead Protrusion ($L$)** | $L$ | Flush to $2.5\\text{ mm}$ (no short) | Flush to **$1.5\\text{ mm}$** | Flush to **$1.5\\text{ mm}$** (lead end discernible in solder) |\n\n![Section 7.3.5: Plated-Through Hole (PTH) Vertical Fill (A) and Wetting Criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec7_tht_vertical_fill.png)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Surface Mount Assemblies Complete Dimensional Criteria Matrix (Section 8)\n\n### 4.1 Rectangular/Square Chip Components (1, 3, or 5-Side Terminations — Section 8.3.2, Table 8-2)\n| Dimension | Description | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | **Maximum Side Overhang** | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | **$\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$** | **$\\le 25\\%\\,W$ or $25\\%\\,P$** (whichever is less) |\n| **B** | **Maximum End Overhang** | **Not permitted ($0$)** | **Not permitted ($0$)** | **Not permitted ($0$)** |\n| **C** | **Minimum End Joint Width** | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | **$\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$** | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,W$ or $75\\%\\,P$** (whichever is less) |\n| **D** | **Minimum Side Joint Length** | Wetting evident | Wetting evident | Wetting evident |\n| **E** | **Maximum Fillet Height** | May overhang land/top of metallization (no contact with body) | Same as Class 1 | Same as Class 1 |\n| **F** | **Minimum Fillet Height** | Proper wetting evident | **$\\ge G + 25\\%\\,H$ or $G + 0.5\\text{ mm}$** (whichever is less) | **$\\ge G + 25\\%\\,H$ or $G + 0.5\\text{ mm}$** (whichever is less) |\n| **G** | **Solder Thickness** | Wetting evident | Wetting evident | Wetting evident |\n\n### 4.2 Flat Gull-Wing Leads (QFP, SOIC, SOT, TSOP — Section 8.3.5, Table 8-5)\n| Dimension | Description | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | **Maximum Side Overhang** | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$ | **$\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$** | **$\\le 25\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$** (for pitch $\\le 0.5\\text{ mm}$, no overhang violating clearance) |\n| **B** | **Maximum Toe Overhang** | Not violating min electrical clearance | Not violating min electrical clearance | Not violating min electrical clearance |\n| **C** | **Minimum End Joint Width** | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ | **$\\ge 50\\%\\,W$** | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,W$** |\n| **D** | **Minimum Side Joint Length** | Wetting evident ($L \\ge 1\\,W$ if $L < 3W$, or $\\ge 1\\,W$) | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,L$** (if $L \\le 3W$) or **$\\ge 3\\,W$** (if $L > 3W$) | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,L$** (if $L \\le 3W$) or **$\\ge 3\\,W$** (if $L > 3W$) |\n| **E** | **Maximum Heel Fillet Height** | Must not contact ceramic/metal package body | May touch plastic SOIC body, not lead bend top if shorting | Must not touch lead knee/body on ceramic/metal |\n| **F** | **Minimum Heel Fillet Height** | Wetting evident | **$G + 50\\%\\,T$** (mid-thickness of lead) | **$G + T$** (top of lead thickness at heel) |\n| **G** | **Solder Thickness** | Wetting evident | Wetting evident | Wetting evident |\n\n### 4.3 J-Leads (PLCC, SOJ — Section 8.3.7, Table 8-7) & BGA Area Array (Section 8.3.12)\n- **J-Leads (Sec 8.3.7)**:\n  - Max Side Overhang ($A$): $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ (Class 1/2), $\\le 25\\%\\,W$ (Class 3).\n  - Min End Joint Width ($C$): $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ (Class 1/2), $\\ge 75\\%\\,W$ (Class 3).\n  - Min Side Joint Length ($D$): $\\ge 1.5\\,W$ (Class 1/2), $\\ge 1.5\\,W$ (Class 3).\n  - Min Heel Fillet Height ($F$): $G + 50\\%\\,T$ (Class 1), $G + T$ (Class 2), $G + 2T$ (Class 3).\n- **BGA Surface Mount Area Array (Sec 8.3.12)**:\n  - **Alignment**: Solder ball offset $\\le 25\\%$ of ball diameter (never violating clearance).\n  - **Voiding (X-Ray Inspection)**: Defect (Class 1, 2, 3) if **voids in any solder ball exceed $25\\%$ of the X-ray image area** ($\\le 50\\%$ diameter of circular projection).\n  - **Head-in-Pillow / Non-wetting / Fractured Ball**: Defect across Classes 1, 2, 3.\n\n![Section 8.3.1.2: Chip components bottom-only termination End Overhang (B) defect.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec8_smt_chip_overhang.png)\n\n![Section 8.3.5: Flat Gull-Wing Leads dimensional parameters (A, B, C, D, E, F, G).](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec8_smt_gullwing_leads.png)\n\n![Section 8.3.7: J-Leads dimensional parameters and heel fillet criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec8_smt_j_leads.png)\n\n![Section 8.3.12: Surface Mount Area Array (BGA) solder connection & voiding criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec8_smt_bga_solder.png)\n\n![Section 9: Component physical damage, chip metallization loss, and body cracks.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec9_component_damage.png)\n\n![Section 10: Printed circuit board laminate measling, crazing, and delamination criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec10_measling_delamination.png)\n\n![Section 11: Discrete wiring and solderless wrap (wire-wrap) turn criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc610_sec11_solderless_wrap.png)\n",
  "ipc-600j": "# 🔬 IPC-A-600J: Acceptability of Printed Boards — Bare Board External & Internal Microsection Atlas\n\n> **Standard**: ANSI/IPC-A-600J (*Acceptability of Printed Boards*, May 2016, 200 Pages, Sections 1–5)\n> **Source PDF**: [894923540-IPC-A-600J-Acceptability-of-Printed-Boards-英文版.pdf](Reading/894923540-IPC-A-600J-Acceptability-of-Printed-Boards-%E8%8B%B1%E6%96%87%E7%89%88.pdf)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scope, Product Classes & Three-Tier Acceptance Criteria (Section 1)\n\nUnlike IPC-A-610 (which covers populated electronic assemblies), **IPC-A-600J** defines visual and metallographic cross-section acceptance criteria for **unpopulated (bare) rigid, flexible, rigid-flex, HDI, and metal-core printed boards**:\n- **Three Acceptance Tiers (Section 1.5)**:\n  1. **Target Condition**: Ideal/preferred manufacturing condition.\n  2. **Acceptable Condition**: Meets IPC-6011/6012/6013 performance specification for Class 1, Class 2, or Class 3.\n  3. **Nonconforming Condition**: Fails to meet or exceeds the maximum allowable defect criteria for the designated Class.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Externally Observable Characteristics (Section 2)\n\n| Section | Characteristic | Class 1 Criteria | Class 2 Criteria | Class 3 Criteria |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **2.1.3** | **Edge Haloing & Nicks** | Penetration $\\le 50\\%$ of edge-to-conductor distance or $\\le 2.5\\text{ mm}$ | Penetration $\\le 50\\%$ of edge-to-conductor distance or $\\le 2.5\\text{ mm}$ | Penetration $\\le 50\\%$ of edge-to-conductor distance or $\\le 2.5\\text{ mm}$ |\n| **2.2.1** | **Weave Exposure / Weave Texture** | Weave exposure acceptable if spacing met | No unbroken glass fibers bridged between conductors | No weave exposure reducing dielectric spacing below minimum |\n| **2.3.1–2.3.3** | **Measling, Crazing & Delamination/Blistering** | Measling acceptable; blistering/delamination $\\le 1\\%$ area, bridges $\\le 25\\%$ conductor spacing | Crazing/blistering $\\le 25\\%$ of distance between plated holes/conductors | **Blistering/delamination bridging $>25\\%$ of dielectric spacing is Nonconforming** |\n| **2.5.3** | **PTH Copper Plating Voids (Visual)** | $\\le 3$ voids per hole; $\\le 10\\%$ of holes | **$\\le 1$ void per hole**; $\\le 5\\%$ of holes; length $\\le 5\\%$ of board thickness, $<90^\\circ$ circumference | **No copper plating voids permitted ($0\\text{ voids}$)** |\n| **2.9** | **Solder Mask Registration & Webs** | Misregistration permitted if electrical function intact | No solder mask onto SMT lands exceeding max encroachment ($0.05\\text{ mm}$); no exposed copper on adjacent traces risking bridging | **Zero solder mask encroachment onto critical wire-bond / fine-pitch lands** |\n| **2.10.1** | **Conductor Width Reduction (Nicks/Pinholes)** | Isolated reduction **$\\le 30\\%$** of minimum conductor width ($W$) | Isolated reduction **$\\le 20\\%$** of minimum width $W$ (length $\\le W$ or $3.0\\text{ mm}$) | Isolated reduction **$\\le 20\\%$** of minimum width $W$ |\n| **2.10.2–2.10.4** | **External Annular Ring (Supported PTH / Unsupported)** | **$90^\\circ$ breakout permitted** if conductor-to-land junction $\\ge 0.05\\text{ mm}$ ($2\\text{ mil}$) or $50\\%\\,W$ | **$90^\\circ$ breakout permitted** in non-critical areas (or $\\ge 0.05\\text{ mm}$ per IPC-6012); Unsupported hole $\\ge 0.15\\text{ mm}$ ($6\\text{ mil}$) | **Minimum $0.05\\text{ mm}$ ($2\\text{ mil}$) external annular ring required ($0^\\circ$ breakout permitted)** |\n| **2.11** | **Bow and Twist (Flatness)** | $\\le 1.5\\%$ (THT) / $\\le 0.75\\%$ (SMT) | **$\\le 0.75\\%$ for boards with SMT components**; $\\le 1.5\\%$ without SMT | **$\\le 0.75\\%$ for SMT**; $\\le 1.5\\%$ without SMT |\n\n![Section 2.1.3: Haloing along board edges and non-plated holes.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec2_1_haloing_burrs.png)\n\n![Section 2.3.1: Subsurface Measling vs. Crazing and Delamination.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec2_3_measling_crazing.png)\n\n![Section 2.5.3: Plated-Through Hole (PTH) visual copper plating voids.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec2_5_pth_copper_voids.png)\n\n![Section 2.9.3: Solder-Mask-Defined (SMD) vs. Copper-Defined (NSMD) BGA Lands.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec2_9_bga_smd_vs_nsmd.png)\n\n![Section 2.10.2 & 2.10.3: External Annular Ring measurement and Supported PTH criteria.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec2_10_ext_annular_ring.png)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Internally Observable Microsection Characteristics (Section 3)\n\nMetallographic cross-sectioning (vertical/axial and horizontal/transverse microsections, often after $288^\\circ\\text{C}$ $10\\text{ s}$ thermal stress testing) evaluates internal multilayer integrity:\n\n### 3.1 Dielectric Etchback, Smear Removal & Resin Recession (Section 3.1.5–3.1.8)\n- **Positive Etchback (Sec 3.1.5.1)**: Controlled chemical removal of resin and glass fibers so inner-layer copper foil protrudes into the PTH barrel for **3-point interconnect bonding** (Acceptable Class 3: $0.005\\text{ mm}$ to $0.080\\text{ mm}$ / $0.2\\text{--}3.15\\text{ mil}$ without shadowing).\n- **Smear Removal / Desmear (Sec 3.1.5.2)**: Complete removal of drill-smeared epoxy resin from the inner-layer copper interconnect face is mandatory across Classes 1, 2, 3.\n- **Negative Etchback (Sec 3.1.5.3)**: Inner-layer copper recessed back from the hole wall; acceptable up to $\\le 0.013\\text{ mm}$ ($0.5\\text{ mil}$) if specified, otherwise nonconforming if excessive.\n- **Resin Recession (Sec 3.1.8)**: Voids between the PTH copper barrel wall and surrounding dielectric after thermal stress: acceptable if $\\le 40\\%$ of total dielectric thickness and not bridging conductively.\n\n![Section 3.1.5: Microsection verification of Positive Etchback, Desmear, and Negative Etchback.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec3_1_etchback_smear.png)\n\n### 3.2 PTH Copper Plating Voids, Wicking, Foil & Barrel Cracks (Section 3.3)\n| Microsection Defect | IPC-A-600J Code / Definition | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Copper Plating Voids (3.3.1)** | Void in PTH barrel wall (evaluated in vertical cross-section) | $\\le 3$ voids; total length $\\le 10\\%$ board thickness | $\\le 1$ void in any plane; length $\\le 5\\%$ of board thickness; none at innerlayer junction | **Zero copper plating voids allowed ($0\\text{ voids}$)** |\n| **Copper Wicking (3.3.4)** | Extension of copper plating along glass fibers into laminate | $\\le 100\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ ($3.94\\text{ mil}$) if spacing met | **$\\le 80\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ ($3.15\\text{ mil}$)** (and $\\le 100\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ into clearance hole) | **$\\le 50\\text{ }\\mu\\text{m}$ ($1.97\\text{ mil}$)** (never violating min dielectric spacing) |\n| **Foil Crack \"A\" / \"B\" (3.3.9)** | \"A\": Crack in external foil only; \"B\": Partial crack not breaking minimum plating | Acceptable | \"A\" Acceptable; \"B\" Nonconforming | \"A\" Acceptable; \"B\" Nonconforming |\n| **Internal Foil \"C\" Crack (3.3.8)** | Crack in internal copper foil at thermal stress zone | Nonconforming | **Nonconforming** | **Nonconforming** |\n| **Barrel \"E\" Crack (3.3.10)** | Crack through PTH copper plating barrel wall | Nonconforming | **Nonconforming** | **Nonconforming** |\n| **Corner \"F\" Crack (3.3.11)** | Crack in PTH copper plating at knee/surface land corner | Nonconforming | **Nonconforming** | **Nonconforming** |\n| **Internal Annular Ring (3.3.12)** | Innerlayer land width around drilled PTH | $90^\\circ$ breakout acceptable | Tangency ($0\\text{ mm}$) or $90^\\circ$ breakout per spec | **$\\ge 0.025\\text{ mm}$ ($1.0\\text{ mil}$) minimum internal annular ring ($0^\\circ$ breakout)** |\n| **Microvia Target Land Contact (3.3.13–3.3.15)** | Laser/photo microvia bottom interface to target pad | Full contact without separation | Full metallic bond; penetration $\\le 50\\%$ of target foil | Full dimple/flat contact; zero separation after thermal stress |\n\n![Section 3.3.1: Microsection Copper Plating Voids in Plated-Through Holes.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec3_3_plating_voids.png)\n\n![Section 3.3.9: Foil and Plating Cracks (A, B, D, E, F cracks) in cross-section.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec3_3_barrel_corner_cracks.png)\n\n![Section 3.3.12: Internal Layer Annular Ring microsection measurement.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec3_3_int_annular_ring.png)\n\n![Section 3.3.14: Microvia Target Land Contact Dimension and Penetration.](Reading/figures/ipc600_sec3_3_microvia_target_land.png)\n"
};
